{
  "$comment": "The sprite face of the wirenook bot, written by blender/bot/build_bot.py; do not edit. The mesh `Body` is the face alone (the sprites and every shape key, on the material `bot`); the rest of the bot, the backing plate included, is the mesh `Shell`, which has no shape keys. The head has a screen: a backing plate behind an opening in the front of the head. Textured quads (sprites) rest either just in front of the plate (shown) or just behind it (hidden), and shape keys on the mesh only ever slide them along the screen's depth. Two layers are independent, `eyes` (the upper rows of the screen) and `mouth` (the lower rows); each has one neutral sprite that rests shown. A key of a layer brings its own copy of a sprite forward to `shownDepth` and sends that layer's neutral sprite back; both cross the plate at weight `depth.crossover`. Where two keys of a layer are on, the sprite with the larger `shownDepth` covers the other (sprites are listed from back to front), so a blink covers an expression and speech covers a smile. `overlay` sprites lie over the layers and hide nothing: the two eyelids (half the eyes layer each), the blush marks and the full-screen bluescreen. A key whose `layer` is null shows nothing; it moves a hidden triangle by `depth.emptyNudge` so that no tool drops it. Left and right are the avatar's own. Lengths are metres. `unity` positions are in the avatar's frame as Unity imports it (x to its right, y up, z forward); `blender` ones are x to its left, y back, z up. `rect` is [x, y, width, height] in texels from the atlas's top left; `uv` is [u0, v0, u1, v1] from its bottom left; `screenRect` is [column, row, width, height] in screen pixels from the screen's top left, as someone facing the avatar sees it.",
